<font face="Arial" size="-1">
<p>
<font size="+1"><b>Example Skill Trees</b></font>
<br>
Below are two examples of skills trees as they will be found in Origin.  Both are very small parts of the whole tree but should give you a good feel of the branching and complexity involved in the trees
<p>
In the first example, we will take a look at blacksmithing.  At the top of the blacksmithing skill tree is a single, starting skill box entitled "Simple Metalworks". By spending two skill points (we call them "ankhs") a character can acquire this skill box enabling him to craft a handful of simple, yet useful items such as mugs, cake pans, and nails. Not only does this skill grant the character the ability to craft some items, it also further opens the blacksmithing skill tree. With the knowledge of Simple Metalworks, the character may now use additional ankhs to purchase "Metal Shield Smithing", "Tool Smithing", or "Blunt Weapon Smithing". Choosing "Blunt Weapon Smithing" opens up other weapon types such as "Sword Smithing" and "Axe Smithing". It also enables the smith to further specialize in the crafting of blunt weapons. 
<p>
Specialization is rewarded within the skill trees. A blacksmith who chooses to specialize in blunt weapons by continuing to spend ankhs on skill boxes within that branch gains the ability to craft a larger variety of maces and hammers. In addition, he also gains the ability to augment their vital statistics such as damage, accuracy and durability in order to forge a truly formidable weapon. 
<p>
The concept of specialization goes beyond that of focusing one's attention on a single branch of a single skill tree. A blacksmith who has mastered the art of blunt weapon smithing may now more easily master other similar branches such as sword smithing, or axe smithing. In this way a character benefits from continuing to spend his ankhs within a given skill tree. That is not to say that it is unwise to diversify. It is possible to build a warrior mage character that is well versed in both magic and combat. While this character gains extra flexibility as a hybrid, he will never be as proficient in magic as a pure mage, or as proficient in combat as a pure warrior. 
<p>
Keep in mind that even if a character chooses to spend all of his ankhs within a given skill tree, he will still most likely never be able to purchase every skill box within it. This is due to the fact that the major skill trees such as combat, magic, tinkering, alchemy, etc. contain more boxes than a character is ever likely to be able to acquire. Because of this, even two characters that have devoted themselves solely to the same skill tree will still differ in their choice of skills. 
<p>
-From Ben Hanson's <a class=post href=http://www.uo2.com/comments-dev_13.html>Team Comments</a>
