</td>
</tr>
</table>
<p>
Our second example is the start of the combat skill tree.  Every character begins with the ability to fight on a very rudimentary level. Even a character with no combat training has the ability to fight with daggers and knives. Although this character will be able to effectively fight only the weakest of monsters, not even the unskilled are completely defenseless. Those who wish to devote ankhs (skill points) to the combat skill tree are given additional combat options. The entry-level skill branch is unarmed combat with the first box being 'Backfist'. This skill enables the character to initiate a backfist maneuver in battle. Before we continue progressing the skill tree, let's take a look at combat maneuvers. 
<p>
Upon obtaining 'Backfist' we may move onto 'Blunt Weapon Use', continue to gain more unarmed combat maneuvers with 'Upper Cut' and 'Front Kick', or increase unarmed combat effectiveness with 'Neophyte's Unarmed Tactics'. If we choose 'Blunt Weapon Use', we may now move onto 'Sword Use' and 'Axe Use'. We may also increase our effectiveness with blunt weapons or begin to advance our basic skill with all melee weapons. If we continue to specialize in blunt weapons, we gain access to more and more powerful combinations specific to this weapon type. We also open up the opportunity to learn the use of more advanced blunt weaponry. 
<p>
Other melee skill branches include Unarmed, Swords, Axes, Knives, and Pole Weapons, each of which allows for the specialization in normal or technological variations. And yes, there's even an advanced specialization involving two-weapon techniques and combinations. There are also a variety of ranged skill branches both normal and technological. In addition to these, we have access to armor and shield skills as well as special actions such as the ability to stun a foe or the ability to augment the combat effectiveness of everyone in the party. 
<p>
Additional skills include 'Jab', a maneuver that is very fast and accurate but only at the expense of damage, 'Whirlwind Strike' which is damaging but leaves the attacker very weak in defense, 'Geoffrey's Polearm In-fighting', a maneuver that is exceptionally good at knocking down opponents in heavy armor, 'Lumberjack's Fury' which is most effective against pole weapons, and 'The Drunken Fist' which grants unarmed ferocity as long as the character is drunk. 
<p>
-From Ben Hanson's <a class=post href=http://www.uo2.com/comments-dev_17.html>Team Comments</a>
